// The sculpt package: procedural modelling, the operators that build a solid
// from a few numbers rather than from a list of vertices. A program reaches it
// with 'import sculpt', and gets:
//
// - Profile, a 2-D outline: a polyline, open or closed, with its generators:
//   a regular polygon, a circle, a rectangle, a rounded rectangle, the convex
//   hull of points, and a polyline sampled from a 2-D Bezier curve; and the
//   functions behind the last two, 'convexHull' and 'sampleCurve' (any of
//   geomath's Bezier curves, 2-D or 3-D, as a List of points).
// - Path, a 3-D polyline sampled from Bezier curves or given as points, and
//   the frames along it: rotation-minimizing frames, by the double-reflection
//   method; 'curveFrames', the same as sdf's CurveFrames, which with
//   'distances' are what an sdf.PathCurve views.
// - Profile to solid: 'extrude' (along a direction), 'lathe' (revolved about
//   an axis) and 'sweep' (along a path, with twist and taper; 'sweepScaled'
//   with the scale any function of arc length), each making a
//   mesh.PolyMesh of quads, with a corner "uv" channel and the vertex
//   channels "along" and "around" (see below).
// - Lofts and bridges: 'loft' (a surface through profiles placed in given
//   frames) and 'loftAlong' (placed along a path, following it), the
//   profiles of any counts and shapes put on common columns and started so
//   the band does not twist, joined linearly or by a Catmull-Rom spline, with
//   rings between and optional caps, writing "uv", "along" and "around" as
//   the other generators do; 'bridgeLoops' (a band of faces joining boundary
//   loops of a mesh in pairs, as Blender's Bridge Edge Loops, with cuts and a
//   smoothness that leaves each loop along its surface) and 'boundaryLoop'.
// - Deformers, which move a PolyMesh's vertices and leave its topology and
//   channels as they were: 'bend', 'twist' and 'taper' (Barr's global
//   deformations, as Blender's Simple Deform modifier offers them),
//   'curveDeform' (a mesh laid along a path, as Blender's Curve modifier),
//   and 'displaceAlongNormal' (each vertex along its normal by a stored
//   channel's value times an amount, as Blender's Displace modifier).
// - Lattice (free-form deformation): 'Lattice', a box spanned by three axes
//   and a grid of l x m x n control points over it, read, set and moved one
//   by one, and 'latticeDeform', each vertex moved by the control points'
//   moves weighted at its place in the box, by Bernstein polynomials
//   (Sederberg and Parry's FFD), trilinearly, or by a clamped uniform cubic
//   B-spline ('latticeWeights' gives the weights), as Blender's Lattice
//   modifier; the topology and every channel are kept.
// - Copies and merging: 'mirror' (the mesh and its reflection in a plane,
//   the copy's faces reversed to stay outward, the seam optionally merged,
//   as Blender's Mirror modifier; 'mirrorWith' also flips or offsets the
//   copy's uvs), the arrays 'arrayLinear', 'arrayRadial' and
//   'arrayAlongPath' (copies under a transform each, concatenated in order,
//   neighbouring copies' touching vertices optionally merged, as Blender's
//   Array modifier), and 'weld' (vertices within a distance merged, each
//   cluster kept as its lowest-numbered vertex, as Blender's Merge by
//   Distance and Weld modifier), each carrying every channel, a merged
//   edge's "crease" the greatest of its edges'.
// - Join: 'join' (separately made meshes as one PolyMesh, part after part,
//   nothing welded, as Blender's Join; 'joinPlaced' places each part first),
//   carrying the union of the parts' channels, matched by name and domain,
//   each part that lacked one filled with the channel's own fill value; the
//   same name with another type or another domain is refused and reported,
//   not converted.
// - Booleans: 'booleanUnion', 'booleanDifference' and 'booleanIntersection'
//   (and 'boolean' and 'booleanWith', which also creases the curve where the
//   surfaces meet) of two closed, manifold meshes, as Blender's Boolean
//   modifier, decided exactly in the manner of the Manifold library: every
//   side, crossing and order an exact sign, ties broken by a symbolic
//   perturbation (coplanar faces of a union overlap, a flush cutter cuts
//   through), the result closed and manifold and the same bits on every
//   machine, only the new vertices rounded, once; channels as 'join' carries
//   them, a new vertex's blended along its edge and a new corner's across its
//   triangle. 'orient2dSign' and 'orient3dSign' are the exact predicates.
// - Voxel remesh: 'voxelRemesh' and 'voxelRemeshWith' (a closed mesh rebuilt
//   as an even, all-quad, closed and manifold surface at a voxel size, as
//   Blender's Voxel Remesh: the solid sampled as a signed distance, inside
//   decided exactly by ray parity, and meshed by sdfmesh's surface nets;
//   overlapping closed parts fuse; channels carried from the nearest point,
//   an edge channel's values its fill; optionally each vertex moved onto the
//   input's surface to keep its volume).
// - Bevel: 'bevelEdges' and 'bevelEdgesWith' (each selected edge replaced by a
//   strip of faces, flat or rounded by a superellipse profile, its width
//   measured as Blender's offset, width or depth, the corners where strips
//   meet mitred, a box's corner patched by quads on the superellipsoid the
//   three profiles bound, the overlap optionally clamped by one factor for
//   every width), 'bevelVertices' and 'bevelVerticesWith' (each selected
//   vertex cut off), as Blender's Bevel; selections are weights per element,
//   from a list, an angle or a channel ("bevel_weight_edge"); every channel
//   carried, a new vertex's values blended by its place, new edges their
//   fill; the strip and corner faces returned for later steps.
// - Face operators on a selection (a list of face numbers): 'extrudeFaces'
//   (each connected region moved along its mean normal, walls of quads on
//   its rim, as Blender's Extrude Region; 'extrudeFacesAlong' by a given
//   vector), 'extrudeIndividual' (each face along its own normal, with its
//   own walls), and 'inset' and 'insetIndividual' (a region, or each face,
//   shrunk inside a border of quads by an even thickness, mitred and
//   clamped, and raised or sunk by a depth, as Blender's Inset Faces), each
//   carrying every channel.
// - Thickness: 'solidify' (a surface and its copy offset along the vertex
//   normals, the copy turned over, with rim quads round each open boundary,
//   placed by an offset from -1 to 1 and optionally of even thickness, as
//   Blender's Solidify modifier in Simple mode; 'solidifyWith' adds its
//   inner, outer and rim creases), carrying every channel.
// - Smoothing, which moves the vertices and leaves the topology and every
//   channel as they were: 'smooth' (the uniform Laplacian, each vertex moved
//   a factor of the way to the mean of its neighbours, all at once each
//   step, as Blender's Smooth modifier) and 'smoothTaubin' (Taubin's
//   lambda|mu, which smooths without shrinking; 'taubinMu' gives mu from the
//   pass-band), a crease or the boundary smoothed only along itself and a
//   corner held, as subdivision holds them; 'smoothWith' and
//   'smoothTaubinWith' pin the boundary or weigh each vertex by a channel.
// - Fewer faces: 'decimate' and 'decimateWith' (edges collapsed, the
//   cheapest first by quadric error metrics, to a ratio or a count of
//   triangles or an error limit, each collapse refused if it would turn a
//   triangle over or break the manifold; boundaries, creases and uv seams
//   kept by penalty planes and seam rules; optional symmetry about an axis
//   plane and a weight channel, as Blender's Decimate modifier in Collapse
//   mode), and 'dissolvePlanar' (neighbouring faces within an angle merged
//   into n-gons and straight runs of vertices dropped, as its Planar mode
//   and Limited Dissolve).
// - Subdivision: 'subdivide', Catmull-Clark, with boundary rules, semi-sharp
//   creases from an edge "crease" and a vertex "crease_vert" channel (as
//   Blender names them), face-varying uvs that keep their seams,
//   and every other channel carried; 'subdivisionLevels', a cage's levels 0
//   to n, a level-of-detail chain. OpenSubdiv keeps subdivision apart from
//   the mesh it refines, and so does this package: a PolyMesh in, a new one
//   out.
// - Fractal refinement of a 2-D outline: 'fractalRefine', each edge split
//   level by level, its split point displaced inside a shrinking
//   quadrilateral (midpoint displacement in Red Blob's noisy-edge form, so
//   the pieces of an edge never cross), every random number a hash of (seed,
//   edge, level, index); the dials in 'Fractal' (roughness, amplitude, a
//   heavy tail, a cascade, a heterogeneity field); 'fractalRefineWithin',
//   only the points whose pieces reach a window, the same bits, for detail
//   made on demand.
// - Ribs as a vertex channel, for displaceAlongNormal, read from "along" and
//   "around": 'ribField', a phase field (a warp, an envelope that fades ribs
//   in patches, each rib its own height or absent, pairs of phase vortices
//   that fork a rib), every term off at its default; and 'marginRing', a ring
//   of cells at the growing rim running an oscillating reaction-diffusion,
//   one pulse a rib (Meinhardt's shells).
//
// Its conventions are geomath's and mesh's, which are the industry's:
// right-handed space, y up, f32, angles in radians, a positive angle turning
// counterclockwise seen from the tip of its axis. A face faces the side from
// which its corners run counterclockwise, and every operator winds its faces
// to face outward when its profile runs counterclockwise (see each one).
// Operators make quads wherever the shape allows, since Catmull-Clark
// subdivision smooths quads best; a cap is one n-gon, and a lathe's faces
// that touch the axis are triangles.
//
// Texture coordinates: every operator gives each face corner a uv in a
// corner channel named "uv", which mesh's 'triangulate' and .obj writer use.
// u runs around (the profile of a sweep or an extrusion, the axis of a
// lathe) and v along (the path, the extrusion, the profile of a lathe), each
// from 0 to 1 in proportion to length. Where a closed loop comes back to its
// start, the corners there get u (or v) = 1, not 0: that is the seam, and
// 'triangulate' splits a vertex whose corners' uvs differ, so a texture wraps
// once without smearing back across the last face.
//
// Generator parameters: extrude, sweep, sweepScaled, lathe and the lofts
// also record each vertex's place in the generator as two f32 vertex
// channels, as Blender's Spline Parameter kept by Capture Attribute: "along", how far the
// profile had travelled (an extrusion's or a sweep's fraction of length, a
// lathe's fraction of its angle), and "around", the fraction of the
// profile's length, each 0 to 1. Every later operator carries them as it
// carries any vertex channel (subdivision by its vertex rules), so after a
// bend a point still knows how far along the straight line it was. A closed
// loop's seam vertex gets 0; the corner "uv" is the seam-correct form.
//
// The algorithms are from the literature; parameters follow Blender's manual,
// never its source:
// - rotation-minimizing frames: Wang, Juttler, Zheng and Liu, "Computation of
//   rotation minimizing frames", ACM Transactions on Graphics 27(1), 2008, the
//   double-reflection method; a closed path's leftover turn is spread along it
//   as twist, as that paper suggests;
// - bend, twist and taper: Barr, "Global and local deformations of solid
//   primitives", SIGGRAPH 1984;
// - lattice: Sederberg and Parry, "Free-form deformation of solid geometric
//   models", SIGGRAPH 1986; the B-spline basis by Cox-de Boor (Piegl and
//   Tiller, The NURBS Book, A2.2);
// - convex hull: Andrew's monotone chain;
// - the loft's spline: Catmull and Rom 1974 at uneven knots, evaluated by
//   Barry and Goldman's pyramid ("A recursive evaluation algorithm for a
//   class of Catmull-Rom splines", SIGGRAPH 1988); a bridge's curve is the
//   cubic Hermite;
// - subdivision: Catmull and Clark 1978; DeRose, Kass and Truong 1998;
//   OpenSubdiv's documented rules (see subdivide.cone);
// - smoothing: Taubin, "A signal processing approach to fair surface
//   design", SIGGRAPH 1995, for lambda|mu and its pass-band;
// - weld's spatial hash: Teschner et al., "Optimized spatial hashing for
//   collision detection of deformable objects", 2003, its cells as wide as
//   the distance, the clusters by union-find, so the result does not depend
//   on the order pairs are found in;
// - decimation: Garland and Heckbert, "Surface simplification using quadric
//   error metrics", SIGGRAPH 1997, with its boundary penalty planes; the
//   manifold test is the link condition (Dey, Edelsbrunner, Guha and
//   Nekhayev, "Topology preserving edge contraction", 1999);
// - booleans: the Manifold library's design (Lalish; symbolic perturbation,
//   manifold output from manifold input), its code not used; exact
//   arithmetic and the orientation predicates by Shewchuk, "Adaptive
//   precision floating-point arithmetic and fast robust geometric
//   predicates", 1997; the perturbation in the manner of Edelsbrunner and
//   Mucke's simulation of simplicity (1990); holes bridged as in Eberly's
//   "Triangulation by ear clipping";
// - voxel remesh: inside and outside by ray parity with Shewchuk's exact
//   predicates (above); the nearest point on a triangle by Ericson,
//   "Real-Time Collision Detection" (2005), 5.1.5; the meshing is Gibson's
//   surface nets, in sdfmesh;
// - bevel: Blender's manual for the options and their meaning (offset,
//   width, depth, segments, profile shape, clamp overlap, loop slide, the
//   vertex mesh), the geometry worked out here: offset lines crossed in each
//   face, the profile a superellipse in the affine frame of its two ends and
//   its corner, a box's corner the superellipsoid of its three mitre points.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   helpers.cone    small vector and list helpers the others use
//   profile.cone    Profile, and its uvs; sampleCurve and convexHull
//   path.cone       Frame and Path, rotation-minimizing frames, and the
//                   frames as sdf's CurveFrames
//   solid.cone      extrude, sweep and lathe, and their "along" and "around"
//   loft.cone       loft, loftAlong, and the correspondence they and bridges
//                   share (orientation, start, common columns, the spline)
//   bridge.cone     bridgeLoops, boundaryLoop
//   deform.cone     bend, twist, taper, curveDeform and displaceAlongNormal
//   lattice.cone    Lattice, latticeDeform, latticeWeights and the three bases
//   copies.cone     mirror, mirrorWith, arrayLinear, arrayRadial, arrayAlongPath
//   weld.cone       weld, and the spatial hash that finds its clusters
//   join.cone       join, joinPlaced, and the union of the parts' channels
//   rebuild.cone    a mesh rebuilt from another's elements, every channel carried
//   boolean.cone    booleanUnion, booleanDifference, booleanIntersection,
//                   boolean, booleanWith: the crossings, inside and outside,
//                   the pieces, merging and cleaning, the result's channels
//   exact.cone      expansions, exact orientations, the symbolic perturbation,
//                   rounding a rational once to f32
//   earclip.cone    exact ear clipping, point in polygon, segments meeting
//   bvh.cone        a tree of boxes over triangles, for the crossings
//   remesh.cone     voxelRemesh, voxelRemeshWith: the band of distances, the
//                   rows' exact signs, the field surface nets meshes, the
//                   nearest point and the channels carried from it
//   bevel.cone      bevelEdges, bevelEdgesWith, bevelVertices,
//                   bevelVerticesWith and the selections: the moved points,
//                   the clamp, the profiles, the corner faces and patches,
//                   the channels by recipe
//   decimate.cone   decimate, decimateWith, decimateTarget, triangleCount;
//                   the quadrics, the collapse rules and the heap
//   dissolve.cone   dissolvePlanar
//   regions.cone    selected faces as regions, their rims and sectors, the
//                   move-and-wall rebuild extrude and inset share, and the
//                   rim's uv strip
//   extrudefaces.cone  extrudeFaces, extrudeFacesAlong, extrudeIndividual
//   inset.cone      inset, insetIndividual, the mitres and their clamp
//   solidify.cone   solidify, solidifyWith, the even-thickness factors
//   smooth.cone     smooth, smoothWith, smoothTaubin, smoothTaubinWith,
//                   taubinMu, and the rule each vertex smooths by
//   subdivide.cone  subdivide, subdivideWith, subdivisionLevels, and the rules
//   refine.cone     one level of subdivision's refinement
//   fractal.cone    Fractal, fractalRefine, fractalRefineWithin
//   ribs.cone       RibField, ribField and its terms; MarginRing, marginRing,
//                   marginPeriod

mod sculpt;

import libc;
import geomath use *;
import collections use *;
import mesh use *;
import noise;
import sdf;
import sdfmesh;
